@flexiarg creativity-commons/nested-places
@title Nested Places
@keywords nested enterprises, layers, ecosystem, real users, Ostrom 8
@audience institution designers, programme designers
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 8 (nested enterprises), as transposed in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016), Table 2 and section "8. Nested enterprises". Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29. Derived from the paper, not mined from runs.
@transposes institutions/nested-enterprises
@status draft 2026-10-29; runs correct it
@references [creativity-commons/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/low-cost-local-conflict-arenas creativity-commons/recognised-right-to-self-organise]

! conclusion:
  Contribution, testing, enforcement, conflict resolution and governance
  are organised in multiple layers of nested Places and agencies, and
  the system is built to take part in an existing ecosystem whose users
  supply the tests of success.

  + context:
    Layering is common; the less obvious point is that many features of
    a working ecosystem must themselves be produced, so there are always
    many Producers organising their relations at several levels.

  + IF:
    An institution is being designed for agents and humans.

  + HOWEVER:
    "Developing a computational treatment of such a system divorced from
    real world applications would be a thankless and ultimately futile
    task."

  + THEN:
    Place the work inside an existing ecosystem, say which layer each
    activity belongs to (a single fix, a branch, a fork, the upstream
    project), and take the measures of success from that ecosystem's
    users rather than from the designers.

  + BECAUSE:
    Real-world use is the only source of tests the designers did not
    write themselves.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Example: in the FUTON Emacs fork the bug reporters are the users who can confirm a fix works for them; that confirmation is a test from outside the design.]
